+# Contract: HTTP API — delta for burn-after-reading and password protection
+
+**Feature**: `002-burn-after-reading-password` | **Date**: 2026-11-20
+
+A **delta** against [001's HTTP contract](../../001-pastebin-service/contracts/http-api.md). Every
+rule there still holds exactly as written; this document states only what is added. Where the two
+documents could be read as disagreeing, 001 wins for existing behaviour and this document wins for
+the two new options.
+
+**No new routes.** The four defined routes are unchanged, and the `404` catch-all is unchanged — the
+new options add no path, and `X-Paste-Password` on an undefined route still has no effect whatsoever
+(FR-060, spec edge case).
+
+**On-disk format is not part of this contract** (see [data-model.md](../data-model.md)). The only
+external compatibility promise is that pastes stored by the previous version keep working (FR-058).
+
+---
+
+## New request header
+
+| Header | Routes | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `X-Paste-Password` | `GET /pastes/<id>`, `GET /pastes/<id>/raw` | The paste's password. Required for a protected paste, ignored for an unprotected one. |
+
+- Sent on `POST /pastes`, `DELETE /pastes/<id>`, or any undefined route, it is inert (FR-045).
+- Supplied **more than once** on a read of a protected paste, it is `403` — ambiguous input is
+  refused, not resolved by picking one occurrence (FR-041).
+- Supplied **empty** on a read of a protected paste, it is `403`: no paste can have an empty
+  password (FR-041).
+
+---
+
+## POST /pastes — create
+
+### Request fields (two added)
+
+| Field | Type | Required | Constraints |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `content` | string | yes | ≤ 65,536 bytes UTF-8 encoded (inclusive); `""` is valid |
+| `expires_in` | integer | no | `1 … 2592000` inclusive; absent means never expires |
+| `burn_after_reading` | boolean | no | `true` or `false` only. Absent is identical to `false` (FR-033) |
+| `password` | string | no | 1 … 128 **characters** inclusive. Absent means unprotected (FR-035) |
+
+Unknown fields are still ignored (FR-007). The two new fields are independent and may be combined in
+any of the four ways (spec assumption).
+
+```http
+POST /pastes HTTP/1.1
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"content": "recovery-code-8842", "burn_after_reading": true, "password": "näme wíth spaces!"}
+```
+
+### Response 201 Created — unchanged
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 201 Created
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"id": "A1b2C3d4E5f6G7h8I9j0K_",
+ "delete_token": "7Hq2…",
+ "url": "/pastes/A1b2C3d4E5f6G7h8I9j0K_"}
+```
+
+Exactly these three fields, whatever the new options were set to. The password is **not** echoed and
+the one-time flag is **not** reported here (FR-037, FR-046).
+
+### Errors (added rows)
+
+| Status | Cause | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `400` | `burn_after_reading` is a string, including `"true"` / `"false"` | FR-034 |
+| `400` | `burn_after_reading` is a number, including `1` and `0` | FR-034 |
+| `400` | `burn_after_reading` is `null`, an array, or an object | FR-034 |
+| `400` | `password` is not a string (number, boolean, `null`, array, object) | FR-036 |
+| `400` | `password` is `""` (zero characters) | FR-036 |
+| `400` | `password` is longer than 128 characters | FR-036 |
+
+Accepted boundaries: `password` of exactly 1 and exactly 128 characters; `burn_after_reading` of
+exactly `true` and exactly `false`. Character count, not byte count — a 128-character multi-byte
+password is accepted (FR-036).
+
+Every message names the offending field, e.g.
+`{"error": "field \"burn_after_reading\" must be a boolean, got string"}` (FR-059).
+
+**All 001 error rows are unchanged, and their precedence is unchanged**: a request that the existing
+rules already reject keeps its existing status even when a new field is also invalid — oversized
+`content` is still `413`, a missing `content` is still `400` naming `content` (FR-039). Nothing is
+stored on any rejection (FR-038).
+
+---
+
+## GET /pastes/{id} — read as JSON
+
+### Request
+
+```http
+GET /pastes/A1b2C3d4E5f6G7h8I9j0K_ HTTP/1.1
+X-Paste-Password: näme wíth spaces!
+```
+
+### Response 200 OK — two fields added
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 200 OK
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"id": "A1b2C3d4E5f6G7h8I9j0K_",
+ "content": "recovery-code-8842",
+ "created_at": "2026-11-20T09:15:00Z",
+ "expires_at": null,
+ "burn_after_reading": true,
+ "protected": true}
+```
+
+| Field | Type | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| `id`, `content`, `created_at`, `expires_at` | — | **Unchanged** names, formats, and meanings (FR-054) |
+| `burn_after_reading` | boolean | Exactly `true` or `false` — never absent, never `null`. `true` exactly when the option was enabled at creation (FR-055) |
+| `protected` | boolean | Exactly `true` or `false`. `true` exactly when a password was set at creation (FR-055) |
+
+`delete_token` is still never present, and neither is the password or anything derived from it
+(FR-003, FR-046).
+
+For a paste stored by the previous version, both new fields read `false` (FR-058).
+
+**If the paste is one-time, this `200` is its only one**: the paste is destroyed as part of serving
+this response, and every later request for it — read or delete — is `404` (FR-047, FR-048).
+
+### Errors (added rows)
+
+| Status | Cause | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `403` | paste exists, is unexpired, is protected, and `X-Paste-Password` is absent | FR-041 |
+| `403` | …the header is present but empty | FR-041 |
+| `403` | …the header does not match | FR-041 |
+| `403` | …the header carries another paste's password | FR-041 |
+| `403` | …the header is supplied more than once | FR-041 |
+| `404` | the paste is one-time and has already been read once | FR-048 |
+| `404` | the paste is one-time and a simultaneous read won the single read | FR-049 |
+
+All 001 `404` rows are unchanged and **continue to take precedence over `403`** (see ordering below).
+
+---
+
+## GET /pastes/{id}/raw — read as plain text
+
+### Response 200 OK — unchanged
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 200 OK
+Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
+Content-Length: 18
+
+recovery-code-8842
+```
+
+The body is the content and nothing else. **No new fields appear here** — `burn_after_reading` and
+`protected` are reported only by the JSON route (FR-056, spec edge case).
+
+### Errors
+
+Identical to `GET /pastes/{id}`, including every added `403` and `404` row above: the password
+requirement and the one-time rule apply to **both** read routes identically (FR-040, FR-047).
+
+A one-time paste is consumed by its first successful read on *either* route — reading it raw once
+means the JSON route then answers `404`, and vice versa.
+
+---
+
+## DELETE /pastes/{id} — delete
+
+**Unchanged.** Authorisation is the `X-Delete-Token` header alone (FR-045).
+
+| Status | Cause | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `204` | correct token — **whether or not** the paste is protected or one-time, and with no password supplied | FR-045, FR-053 |
+| `403` | paste exists and is unexpired, but the token is missing, empty, or wrong — **even if a correct `X-Paste-Password` is supplied** | FR-045 |
+| `404` | the paste is one-time and has already been read — **even with the original token** | FR-048 |
+
+A password is never sufficient to delete, and a delete token is never sufficient to read. The two
+credentials do not substitute for each other (FR-045).
+
+---
+
+## Ordering rule (contractual, extends 001)
+
+Both read routes resolve a request in exactly this order:
+
+```text
+1. identifier shape, existence, expiry   → 404
+2. password                               → 403
+3. one-time claim (one-time pastes only)  → 404 if a simultaneous read won it
+4. serve 200
+```
+
+| Guarantee | Consequence | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Step 1 precedes step 2 | `404` always beats `403`. An unknown, deleted, expired, or ill-shaped identifier answers `404` whatever password is supplied, so no response discloses which identifiers exist or which are protected | FR-042, SC-014 |
+| Step 2 precedes step 3 | A refused read consumes nothing. A one-time **and** protected paste survives any number of wrong-password attempts and is still readable once with the right one | FR-050, SC-019 |
+| Step 3 precedes step 4 | Of several simultaneous reads of a one-time paste, exactly one is served the complete content and the rest get `404`. No read returns partial or corrupt content | FR-049, SC-012 |
+| Expiry precedes the one-time rule | An expired one-time paste answers `404` and is never treated as having been read | FR-052 |
+
+Password comparison is constant-time and reveals nothing about the stored value through response
+timing (FR-043). The stored form is not reversible to the original password (FR-046).
+
+---
+
+## Status code summary (delta)
+
+| Status | Added cases |
+|---|---|
+| `200` | the single successful read of a one-time paste; any read of a protected paste with the correct password |
+| `400` | `burn_after_reading` not a boolean; `password` not a string, empty, or over 128 characters |
+| `403` | existing unexpired protected paste, with a missing, empty, wrong, duplicated, or foreign password |
+| `404` | one-time paste already read (read **or** delete), or a simultaneous read lost the single read |
+| `201`, `204`, `413` | no new cases |
+
+Every one of these, like every error in 001, has body `{"error": "<message>"}` with
+`Content-Type: application/json`, and the message names what was wrong specifically enough to act on
+(FR-059).
+
+## Robustness guarantee (unchanged)
+
+No request — malformed new option, missing-password read, consumed-paste read, or concurrent burn —
+causes the service to stop accepting requests (SC-021). `500` remains a backstop that no documented
+input produces.
